ches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\nprint(\"Brasileirao:\", len(rows), \"seasons:\", sorted({r['season'] for r in rows}))\nprint(\"sample:\", rows[0])\nprint(\"teams sample:\", sorted({r['home_team'] for r in rows})[:10])\n# missing goals?\nprint(\"missing home_goal:\", sum(1 for r in rows if r['home_goal']=='' or r['away_goal']==''))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788239317041,"end":1788239317046}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The MCP 2.x API works like FastMCP with `@server.tool()` decorators and `run_stdio_async()` etc. \n\nNow let me plan the implementation:\n\n**Architecture** (pure Python, stdlib + mcp SDK):\n\n```\n├── server.py                  # MCP server entry point (stdio) — registers all tools\n├── soccer/                    # Package\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── models.py              # Dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamStats\n│   ├── normalization.py       # Team name normalization, date parsing, unicode handling\n│   ├── loaders.py             # CSV loaders for all 6 files\n│   ├── graph.py               # Knowledge graph (nodes + edges)\n│   ├── queries.py             # Query engine: match/team/player/competition/statistical queries\n└── tests/\n    ├── test_bdd_match_queries.py      # GWT-style\n    ├── test_bdd_team_queries.py\n    ├── test_bdd_player_queries.py\n    ├── test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n    ├── test_bdd_statistics.py\n    ├── test_bdd_mcp_tools.py  # End-to-end tool tests\n```\n\nLet me look at the data more closely to understand each file's quirks:\n\n1. **Brasileirao_Matches.csv**: `datetime,home_team,home_team_state,away_team,away_team_state,home_goal,away_goal,season,round` — teams like \"Palmeiras-SP\". 4180 matches. Seasons 2012-2024 maybe.\n\n2. **Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv**: `round,datetime,home_team,away_team,home_goal,away_goal,season` — team names like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\", \"América - MG\". Round as string.\n\n3. **Libertadores_Matches.csv**: `datetime,home_team,away_team,home_goal,away_goal,season,stage` — goals are strings like \"2\". Teams like \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Boca Juniors\".\n\n4. **BR-Football-Dataset.csv**: `tournament,home,home_goal,away_goal,away,home_corner,...,time,date,ht_diff,at_diff,ht_result,at_result,total_corners` — goals floats like 1.0. date is ISO \"2023-09-24\". tournaments like \"Copa do Brasil\". Note: this file has no `season` column — season must be derived from date. Also might have missing values.\n\n5. **novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv**: `ID,Data,Ano,Rodada,Equipe_mandante,Equipe_visitante,Gols_mandante,Gols_visitante,Mandante_UF,Visitante_UF,Vencedor,Arena,OBS` — Data is DD/MM/YYYY. Teams: \"Guarani\", \"Athletico-PR\", \"Grêmio\". 6886 matches 2003-2019.\n\n6. **fifa_data.csv**: first column is unnamed index `,ID,Name,Age,Photo,Nationality,Flag,Overall,Potential,Club,Club Logo,Value,Wage,Special,Preferred Foot,International Reputation,Weak Foot,Skill Moves,Work Rate,Body Type,Real Face,Position,Jersey Number,Joined,Loaned From,Contract Valid Until,Height,Weight,LS,ST,...` — 18207 players. BOM at start (﻿). Note column \"\" (empty first header).\n\nLet me now examine data quirks more carefully — checking unique team names, date formats, missing values, etc. Let me do quick pandas-free exploration (pandas is NOT installed — only pytest, coverage. So I must use stdlib csv module. Good — keeps it dependency-light).\n\nImportant checks:\n- Brasileirao: how are goals stored (int), datetime format\n- Brazilian_Cup: \"round\" values, are there missing goals?\n- Libertadores: goals as strings, missing values?\n- BR-Football: floats, NaNs\n- novo: date format DD/MM/YYYY\n- fifa: BOM, \"Club\" may be empty for free agents\n\nLet me explore with python quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b5e84bb001lslsRjuAZwyJ7E","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b5e64730018RdYsRbpCNitAY"}}
