mio'\n    38 'Grêmio Prudente'\n   130 'Guarani'\n   628 'Internacional'\n    38 'Ipatinga'\n    38 'Joinville'\n   210 'Juventude'\n   190 'Náutico'\n   582 'Palmeiras'\n   248 'Paraná'\n   134 'Paysandu'\n   362 'Ponte Preta'\n   114 'Portuguesa'\n    76 'Santa Cruz'\n    38 'Santo André'\n   666 'Santos'\n   342 'Sport'\n   172 'São Caetano'\n   666 'São Paulo'\n   552 'Vasco'\n   396 'Vitória'\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\np='data/kaggle/'\nc=collections.Counter()\nwith open(p+'novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n        c[r['Equipe_mandante']]+=1; c[r['Equipe_visitante']]+=1\nprint(\"novo teams:\", len(c))\nfor t,n in sorted(c.items()): print(f\"  {n:4d} {t!r}\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788239407443,"end":1788239407446}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a clear picture of the naming across all files. Let me also check `novo` distinct team names and the FIFA Brazilian clubs:\n\nFIFA Brazilian clubs (from the check): Grêmio, Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, América FC (Minas Gerais), Botafogo, Bahia, Paraná, Atlético Paranaense, Vitória, Sport Club do Recife, Chapecoense, Ceará Sporting Club. Let me get the full list of Brazilian clubs in FIFA data.\n\nNow, the normalization design. The core problem set:\n\n**Canonical Brazilian clubs registry** (canonical id → aliases found in data):\n\nFor the major clubs, I need a mapping. Rather than enumerating every single small club perfectly (impossible), I'll:\n1. Build a `CANONICAL_TEAMS` table with curated canonical names for the well-known clubs (those in the spec examples + all clubs appearing across multiple files), each with a set of alias patterns.\n2. Apply a multi-stage matching strategy:\n   - exact alias match (after aggressive normalization: deaccent, lowercase, strip state suffix, strip parentheticals, strip punctuation)\n   - suffix/stripping match (e.g. \"vasco da gama rj\" → strip trailing UF)\n   - curated alias table\n\nKey aliases needed (normalized forms → canonical):\n\n- **flamengo**: flamengo, flamengo rj — but NOT \"flamengo do piaui\"/\"flamengo pi\" (distinct club).\n- **fluminense**: fluminense, fluminense rj — not \"fluminense de feira\"/\"fluminense pi\".\n- **palmeiras**: palmeiras (all files use \"Palmeiras\").\n- **corinthians**: corinthians.\n- **sao paulo**: sao paulo.\n- **santos**: santos — not \"santos ap\", \"santos laguna\".\n- **gremio**: gremio, gremio rs — not \"gremio novorizontino\" etc.\n- **atletico_mg**: atletico mg, atletico mineiro, atletico (bare in Libertadores!?) — wait, bare \"Atletico\" doesn't appear in Libertadores... Actually from the Libertadores list: 'Atlético-MG' (76), 'Atlético Nacional', 'Atlético Tucumán', 'Atlético-PR', 'Athletico' (29 bare). So \"Athletico\" bare = Athletico Paranaense (they renamed from Atlético-PR to Athletico-PR in 2019 — the Libertadores dataset dropped the suffix for them, or it's \"Athletico Paranaense\" shortened). Hmm, \"Athletico\" bare (29) — Athletico Paranaense played Libertadores in 2019, 2020 (wait, 2020 and 2022). The dataset has 'Athletico' and separately 'Atlético-PR' (14) and 'Atlético Paranaense'... wait no, in Libertadores there's no \"Atlético Paranaense\". OK so bare \"Athletico\" in Libertadores = Athletico Paranaense. Good — alias `athletico` → Athletico-PR? But bare \"Atletico\" in BR-Football = Atletico Mineiro (424 'Atletico Mineiro' separate, and 'Atletico' bare doesn't appear in BR-Football... wait, from my normalized count earlier, bare \"atletico\" appeared 1984 times — that was AFTER stripping the state suffix from \"Atletico-MG\", \"Atletico-GO\", \"Atletico-PR\" etc. So bare \"atletico\" never appears raw. And bare \"athletico\" (657) came from stripping \"-PR\" from \"Athletico-PR\"... no wait, Brasileirao uses \"Atletico-PR\". Cup uses \"Atlético - PR\" and \"Athletico Paranaense - PR\". BR-Football uses \"Athletico Paranaense\" and \"Atletico Paranaense\". Libertadores uses bare \"Athletico\". novo uses \"Athletico-PR\" (2019+ seasons; before that \"Atletico-PR\" presumably).\n\nSo: **rule**: within Libertadores, bare \"Athletico\" → Athletico Paranaense. I'll add alias \"athletico\" → Athletico-PR only in the Libertadores context... Actually simpler: make \"athletico\" a global alias for Athletico Paranaense. Is there any other \"Athletico\" bare elsewhere? BR-Football has \"Athletic Club MG\" (different club, Athletic Club). The bare \"Athletico\" raw string only appears in Libertadores. So a global alias \"athletico\" → Athletico Paranaense is safe.\n\nSimilarly bare \"Atletico\" — does the raw string \"Atletico\"/\"Atlético\" appear anywhere alone? From the raw dumps: Libertadores has 'Atlético-MG', 'Atlético-PR' (with suffix), 'Atlético Nacional', 'Atlético Tucumán', 'Real Atlético'. No bare \"Atletico\". Good.\n\nBut wait — normalized \"atletico\" 1984 came from where? \"Atletico-MG\" → strip suffix → \"atletico\"... no wait, my norm function strips \"-mg\" so \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico\" and \"Atletico-GO\" → \"atletico\" and \"Atletico-PR\" → \"atletico\" — they all collapse to bare \"atletico\"! That's the bug in the naive approach: \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-GO\" and \"Atletico-PR\" all normalize to \"atletico\". That's why \"atletico\" had 1984 counts. So my normalization MUST NOT blindly strip state suffixes when the base name is ambiguous!\n\nBetter approach: **don't strip state suffixes blindly**. Instead:\n- Keep the full normalized string WITH the state (\"atletico mg\", \"atletico pr\", \"atletico go\").\n- A team alias registry maps both \"atletico mg\" AND \"atletico mineiro\" → Atlético-MG.\n- When user queries \"Atlético-MG\" or \"Atlético Mineiro\" or \"Atletico-MG\" → matches.\n- The state suffix is PART of the identity for ambiguous names.\n\nBut then \"Flamengo-RJ\" vs \"Flamengo\": both → \"flamengo\" base with suffix... For query \"Flamengo\", we want to match \"Flamengo-RJ\", \"Flamengo - RJ\", \"Flamengo\" but NOT \"Flamengo-PI\". Strategy: normalization produces `(base, state)` where state is the trailing UF if present. Matching: a query for team T matches a raw team R if base(R) == base(T) AND (state(R) is None OR state(T) is None OR state(R) == state(T))... but \"Flamengo-PI\" has base \"flamengo\" + state PI, and query \"flamengo\" (no state) would match it. Hmm.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach used by many: curated canonical registry with explicit alias sets per club:\n\n```python\nTEAM_ALIASES = {\n  \"flamengo\": {\"flamengo\", \"flamengo rj\", \"flamengo de rio de janeiro\"},\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nEach canonical club has a canonical display name (with state, e.g. \"Flamengo (RJ)\") and a set of normalized aliases explicitly listed. Any raw name from any file gets normalized (deaccent/lowercase/remove punct/paretheticals, remove trailing \"- uf\" or \"- uf\" with spaces, \"da gama\" handling) into a key; if that key ∈ aliases of exactly one club → mapped. The state suffix is STRIPPED during normalization (since aliases include the base name), but clubs whose base names collide (atletico, america, botafogo, vitoria, nacional, sport...) are distinguished by their full alias sets: e.g. \"atletico mg\" includes \"atletico mineiro\" but NOT \"atletico\"; \"atletico pr\" includes \"athletico paranaense\", \"athletico\"... \n\nWait, but if we strip \"-pr\" from \"Atletico-PR\" we get \"atletico\", and \"atletico\" must belong to... which club? Raw \"Atletico-PR\" appears in Brasileirao/Libertadores; raw \"Atletico-MG\" in Libertadores; \"Atletico-GO\". If we strip suffixes we can't tell them apart. So the rule must be: **normalize WITHOUT stripping the state, keep both forms**: key = \"atletico pr\" (with state) as the primary identity. Then \"Atlético Paranaense\" (BR-Football, no state) normalizes to \"atletico paranaense\" — alias it to \"atletico pr\". And bare \"Athletico\" (Libertadores) → alias to \"atletico pr\" too. And \"Athletico-PR\" (novo) → \"athletico pr\" → alias → atletico pr. And \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico mg\"; \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"atletico mineiro\" → alias → atletico mg.\n\nFor clubs WITHOUT ambiguity in their base (flamengo, palmeiras, corinthians, etc.), the base alone suffices, and states are stripped: \"flamengo rj\", \"flamengo pi\" both → base \"flamengo\"... but they're different clubs! \"Flamengo - PI\" (Flamengo do Piauí) vs \"Flamengo - RJ\". Both normalize with state kept: \"flamengo rj\", \"flamengo pi\". Alias \"flamengo rj\" → Flamengo; \"flamengo pi\", \"flamengo do piaui\" → Flamengo-PI. For user query \"flamengo\", ambiguity resolution: match clubs whose normalized name starts with \"flamengo\"... The pragmatic approach for query matching:\n\n**Query matching semantics**: user says \"Flamengo\" → we look up clubs where one of the aliases is exactly \"flamengo\" → Flamengo (RJ). If no exact match, do prefix/substring search across aliases and canonical names, return candidates or pick best. If multiple candidates match equally (e.g. \"Atletico\" → atletico mg? atletico pr? atletico go?), return a disambiguation list — but better to have a sensible default: since \"atletico\" alone isn't an alias of any club, the matcher falls to prefix matches listing Atletico-MG, Atletico-PR, Atletico-GO... For an MCP tool serving an LLM, returning the top match with alternates noted is the best UX. But honestly, for the spec's purposes, queries will use specific names like \"Palmeiras\", \"Flamengo\", \"Corinthians\", which are unambiguous.\n\nI'll implement a tiered matcher:\n1. **Exact alias** (after full normalization incl. state): canonical.\n2. **Base-name match**: strip state from query; if exactly one club has that base alias, use it. (Handles \"Flamengo\" → Flamengo-RJ since only one club has base \"flamengo\"... wait, Flamengo-PI has base \"flamengo\" too!). Hmm. OK: if multiple, prefer the one with more matches in data / the \"major\" club (I'll mark major clubs in the registry — big clubs have their base alias claimed exclusively; minor variants like \"flamengo pi\" get claimed by the minor club, and base \"flamengo\" claimed by the major club).\n\nSo the registry design:\n\n```python\nCANONICAL_CLUBS = {\n    \"flamengo\": {  # key\n        \"name\": \"Flamengo\",\n        \"state\": \"RJ\",\n        \"aliases\": {\"flamengo\", \"flamengo rj\", ...},  # \"flamengo\" base belongs EXCLUSIVELY to this club\n    },\n    \"flamengo_pi\": {\n        \"name\": \"Flamengo-PI\",\n        \"aliases\": {\"flamengo pi\", \"flamengo do piaui\"},\n    },\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nBase aliases (\"flamengo\") are assigned to the major club. Minor same-name clubs must always carry their state/full name.\n\nThis is a lot of curation but it's the core value of the task (\"Handles team name variations correctly\" is an explicit success criterion). Let me build the registry covering all clubs that appear in the main files. 497 normalized distinct names is a lot, but most are unambiguous minor clubs that appear in only one file — for those, the identity can be their own normalized name. The curated registry only needs:\n\n1. All clubs appearing in **multiple files** (where cross-file identity matters).\n2. All **major** Brazilian clubs.\n3. FIFA club name mappings for Brazilian clubs.\n\nLet me define the curated set (canonical_id, display, state, aliases). Let me generate the list of normalized names that appear in ≥2 source files to see the true cross-file curation scope. Also check `novo` distinct raw names.\n\nActually, let me think about the architecture more broadly first, then do the curation systematically.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/          # hmm, keep flat since \"Write all code files to the current directory\"\n```\n\nThe task says \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" — so keep the tree in cwd. I'll use a package dir + server.py at top level.\n\n```\n├── server.py                    # MCP server (stdio) — tools wired to engine\n├── soccer_mcp/\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── context_block.py?        # no — context block comments go inline per file\n│   ├── models.py                # dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamRecord\n│   ├── normalize.py             # team-name normalization + club registry + date parsing\n│   ├── knowledge_graph.py      # build KG (nodes/edges) from matches+players\n│   ├── loaders.py              # CSV loaders for the 6 files\n│   ├── engine.py                # query engine: matches/teams/players/competitions/stats\n├── tests/\n│   ├── conftest.py\n│   ├── test_match_queries.py   # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_team_queries.py\n│   ├── test_player_queries.py\n│   ├── test_competition_queries.py\n│   ├── test_statistics.py\n│   ├── test_normalization.py\n│   └── test_mcp_server.py      # MCP tool-level tests\n├── requirements.txt\n├── pytest.ini / pyproject.toml\n└── README.md (update)\n```\n\n**Models** (`models.py`):\n- `Match`: date (datetime.date), competition, season, stage/round, home_team, away_team (canonical ids), home_team_display, away_team_display, home_goals, away_goals, source_file, plus optional stats (corners, shots, attacks, halftime) from BR-Football.\n- `Player`: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, height, weight, value, preferred foot, skills subset.\n- `TeamRecord`: computed W/D/L/GF/GA/points.\n\n**Normalization** (`normalize.py`):\n- `strip_accents(s)`, `normalize_text(s)`\n- `parse_date(s)` handling ISO, ISO+time, DD/MM/YYYY.\n- `CANONICAL_CLUBS` registry + `canonicalize_team(raw) -> TeamRef or None` and `resolve_team(query) -> list[TeamRef]` (tiered matching).\n- Club registry: I'll curate ~120 Brazilian clubs covering everything in Brasileirao + cross-file clubs + FIFA clubs. For unmatched names, create a synthesized club from the normalized raw name (so Libertadores foreign teams still work: \"boca juniors\", \"river plate\" etc. — they just pass through).\n\nThe matcher tiers for `resolve_team(query)`:\n1. normalize query → if in alias map → that club.\n2. remove state suffix from query → if in alias map → that club (e.g. query \"flamengo rj\" → strip → \"flamengo\" → Flamengo). Actually since aliases include the state variants, tier 1 catches it.\n3. substring/prefix search over aliases → list candidates sorted by match count (popularity = total matches in data) → best candidate if a clear winner, plus report alternates.\n\n**Knowledge graph** (`knowledge_graph.py`): the spec says \"knowledge graph interface\". I'll build an actual in-memory KG:\n- Nodes: Team, Player, Match, Competition, Season?\n- Edges: MATCH_PLAYED_IN(competition), HOME_TEAM(match→team), AWAY_TEAM, WINNER, PLAYER_CLUB(player→team), PLAYER_NATIONALITY...\n- Graph query helpers: neighbors of team (competitions played in, opponents faced), degree counts. Provide `graph_summary`, `team_neighbors` tools. This satisfies the \"knowledge graph interface\" wording without Neo4j (the spec's references to Neo4j appear only in prompts.txt which I'm told to ignore; TASK.md never mandates Neo4j — it says MCP server with knowledge graph interface. An in-memory property graph is a knowledge graph. Also Neo4j isn't installable offline... let me check if neo4j pip package is available. Probably not without network. The venv pip install mcp worked earlier — wait, did it? The output was truncated but showed no error. Network seems available! pip installed mcp 2.1.1. So maybe neo4j driver is installable — but a Neo4j *server* would need to be running. No server available. So in-memory graph it is. That's a justified engineering decision to document in README.)\n\n**Loaders** (`loaders.py`): stdlib csv, typed parsing, per-file mapping to Match; player loader with BOM handling (`utf-8-sig`), empty-club handling; robust int/float parsing (\"NA\", \"\", None).\n\n**Engine** (`engine.py`): SoccerData class holding all matches + players + KG + prebuilt indexes:\n- `find_matches(team=, opponent=, competition=, season=, date_from=, date_to=, stage=, limit=)` \n- `head_to_head(team_a, team_b)` → matches list + W/D/L per team + goals.\n- `team_stats(team, season=, competition=, venue=)` → record.\n- `standings(competition, season)` → table computed (points, W/D/L, GF, GA, GD); champion; relegation (bottom 4).\n- `top_scorers` — not inferable (no scorer data) → provide goals-by-team instead; document limitation.\n- `players_by_name(name)`, `players_by_nationality`, `players_by_club(club)`, `top_players(...)`, `brazilian_players_summary`.\n- `biggest_wins(competition=, season=, n=)` → sorted by goal margin.\n- `average_goals(competition=, season=)`, `home_win_rate(...)`.\n- `derbies(season=)` — known rivalries pairs: Fla-Flu, Grenal (Grêmio vs Internacional), Clássico Majestoso (Corinthians vs Palmeiras), Choque-Rei (São Paulo vs Corinthians, Palmeiras vs São Paulo = Choque-Rei is SPFC x Corinthians? Actually: Derby Paulista = Corinthians x Palmeiras; Majestoso = Corinthians x São Paulo; Choque-Rei = Palmeiras x São Paulo; Clássico dos Milhões = Vasco x Flamengo; Clássico Vovô = Fluminense x Vasco... I'll include a set of ~15 well-known derby pairs).\n- `competitions_for_team(team)`.\n- `seasons_for_competition(competition)`.\n- `graph_summary()` — node/edge counts by type.\n- `team_graph(team)` — KG neighborhood: competitions, opponents (with match counts), players (from FIFA if club matched).\n\n**Performance**: preload everything at startup into memory + prebuilt indexes (dict team_id → match list, competition+season → matches). All queries are O(matches) scans at worst (~24k matches) — well under 2s.\n\n**Deduplication**: BR-Football overlaps with the other files (e.g. Serie A 2014-2023, Copa do Brasil matches). For queries, should I dedupe? The spec treats files as sources; match queries should probably include source attribution but not dedupe across files (BR-Football has extra stats). For standings, I should compute from a specific source (Brasileirao file for Serie A; for \"who won 2019 Brasileirao\" the Brasileirao file covers 2012-2021 → 2019 ✓). I'll keep all matches tagged with `source` and let match searches cover all files; standings use a specified competition mapping (Serie A → primarily Brasileirao_Matches for 2012-2021 + novo for 2003-2019... they overlap 2012-2019! Both have Serie A 2012-2019. Dedupe needed for standings: use Brasileirao file as primary, novo for 2003-2011, BR-Football as fallback. Hmm — simpler: standings tool takes competition+season, selects matches from the BEST source for that season (priority: Brasileirao_Matches > novo_campeonato > BR-Football for Serie A; Cup_Matches > BR-Football for Copa do Brasil; Libertadores for Libertadores; BR-Football for Serie B/C), dedupes by (date, home, away) if mixing. That gives clean standings.\n\nLet me now check the `novo` raw team list and FIFA Brazilian clubs to complete the curation. Also check Cup/Libertadores \"final\" rounds/stages for \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" queries — Cup rounds are 1-8 numeric... hmm, \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — the Cup file has rounds 1-8; final = round 8? With 1337 matches over 2012-2021 (10 seasons) — round 8 would be... let me check what round the final is. And Libertadores has stage \"final\" (15). Good.\n\nLet me look at novo teams + cup round semantics + check a couple of consistency things.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b5fe67e001QDpd1CCSsodl9Z","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b5f3d0b001gB3D0qDEhY16pe"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788239409127,"sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-824f7682e29bc34e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\np='data/kaggle/'\n# Cup rounds semantics: matches per season per round\nwith open(p+'Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\nper = collections.Counter((r['season'], r['round']) for r in rows)\ns='2019'\nprint(\"2019 cup rounds:\", sorted((int(k[1]), v) for k,v in per.items() if k[0]==s))\n# NA goals count\nprint(\"NA goals rows:\", sum(1 for r in rows if 'NA' in (r['home_goal'], r['away_goal'])))\n# Libertadores final sample\nwith open(p+'Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    lrows=[r for r in csv.DictReader(f) if r['stage']=='final']\nprint(\"Lib finals:\", len(lrows))\nfor r in lrows[:6]: print(\"  \", r['datetime'], r['home_team'], r['home_goal'], '-', r['away_goal'], r['away_team'], r['season'])\n# NA season rows in Lib\nwith open(p+'Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    allrows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\nprint(\"Lib NA season:\", sum(1 for r in allrows if r['season']=='NA'), \"NA goals:\", sum(1 for r in allrows if 'NA' in (r['home_goal'],r['away_goal'])))\nEOF","workdir":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-8c2c162e81b7"},"output":"2019 cup rounds: [(1, 40), (2, 20), (3, 20), (4, 10), (5, 16), (6, 8), (7, 4), (8, 2)]\nNA goals rows: 16\nLib finals: 15\n   2013-07-17 21:50:00 Olimpia-PAR 2 - 0 Atlético-MG 2013\n   2013-07-24 21:50:00 Atlético-MG 2 - 0 Olimpia-PAR 2013\n   2014-08-06 21:15:00 Nacional (PAR) 1 - 1 San Lorenzo 2014\n   2014-08-13 21:15:00 San Lorenzo 1 - 0 Nacional (PAR) 2014\n   2015-07-29 22:00:00 Tigres 0 - 0 River Plate 2015\n   2015-08-05 22:00:00 River Plate 3 - 0 Tigres 2015\nLib NA season: 1 NA goals: 0\n","metadata":{"output":"2019 cup rounds: [(1, 40), (2, 20), (3, 20), (4, 10), (5, 16), (6, 8), (7, 4), (8, 2)]\nNA goals rows: 16\nLib finals: 15\n   2013-07-17 21:50:00 Olimpia-PAR 2 - 0 Atlético-MG 2013\n   2013-07-24 21:50:00 Atlético-MG 2 - 0 Olimpia-PAR 2013\n   2014-08-06 21:15:00 Nacional (PAR) 1 - 1 San Lorenzo 2014\n   2014-08-13 21:15:00 San Lorenzo 1 - 0 Nacional (PAR) 2014\n   2015-07-29 22:00:00 Tigres 0 - 0 River Plate 2015\n   2015-08-05 22:00:00 River Plate 3 - 0 Tigres 2015\nLib NA season: 1 NA goals: 0\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\np='data/kaggle/'\n# Cup rounds semantics: matches per season per round\nwith open(p+'Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\nper = collections.Counter((r['season'], r['round']) for r in rows)\ns='2019'\nprint(\"2019 cup rounds:\", sorted((int(k[1]), v) for k,v in per.items() if k[0]==s))\n# NA goals count\nprint(\"NA goals rows:\", sum(1 for r in rows if 'NA' in (r['home_goal'], r['away_goal'])))\n# Libertadores final sample\nwith open(p+'Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    lrows=[r for r in csv.DictReader(f) if r['stage']=='final']\nprint(\"Lib finals:\", len(lrows))\nfor r in lrows[:6]: print(\"  \", r['datetime'], r['home_team'], r['home_goal'], '-', r['away_goal'], r['away_team'], r['season'])\n# NA season rows in Lib\nwith open(p+'Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    allrows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\nprint(\"Lib NA season:\", sum(1 for r in allrows if r['season']=='NA'), \"NA goals:\", sum(1 for r in allrows if 'NA' in (r['home_goal'],r['away_goal'])))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788239409123,"end":1788239409126}},"id":"prt_05b5fe93a001Bw4GADSWVtMWwO","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b5f3d0b001gB3D0qDEhY16pe"}}
