{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788239579775,"sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8e082c566c0554bf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.list_tools))\nsrc = inspect.getsource(MCPServer.call_tool)\nprint(src[:1500])\nEOF","workdir":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-8c2c162e81b7"},"output":"(self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\n(self) -> 'list[MCPTool]'\n    async def call_tool(\n        self, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any], context: Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None = None\n    ) -> CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult:\n        \"\"\"Call a tool by name with arguments.\n\n        Raises:\n            ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n                tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n            UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n                its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n                exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n            MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n        \"\"\"\n        if context is None:\n            context = Context(mcp_server=self, subscriptions=self._subscriptions)\n        return await self._tool_manager.call_tool(name, arguments, context, convert_result=True)\n\n","metadata":{"output":"(self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\n(self) -> 'list[MCPTool]'\n    async def call_tool(\n        self, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any], context: Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None = None\n    ) -> CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult:\n        \"\"\"Call a tool by name with arguments.\n\n        Raises:\n            ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n                tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n            UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n                its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n                exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n            MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n        \"\"\"\n        if context is None:\n            context = Context(mcp_server=self, subscriptions=self._subscriptions)\n        return await self._tool_manager.call_tool(name, arguments, context, convert_result=True)\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.list_tools))\nsrc = inspect.getsource(MCPServer.call_tool)\nprint(src[:1500])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788239579737,"end":1788239579774}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Cup \"final\" round varies by season: in 2012 it's round 6, in 2016 round 7, 2019 round 8, 2021 data incomplete (only up to round 4...). So \"find finals\" logic: the final round = the LAST round (max round) of a season IF it has ≤2 matches (two-legged final) — heuristic: final = matches in the max round of a season when that round has exactly 2 matches between the same pair of teams. I'll implement: for each season, group by round; the final = max round where 2 matches between same two clubs. If max round has 4 matches (semis), no final in data.\n\n2019 Cup final: Athletico Paranaense vs Internacional — Athletico won (real: Athletico won 2019 Copa do Brasil ✓ aggregate 1-0, 2-1 → 3-1). My aggregate logic: leg1 Athletico 1-0 Inter; leg2 Inter 1-2 Athletico. Athletico aggregate 3-1. ✓.\n\nNow I have full understanding of the data. Time to build. Let me now design the file layout and write the code. I'll create:\n\n```\n├── server.py                        # MCP server entry (stdio), registers tools\n├── soccer_mcp/\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── models.py                   # Match, Player, TeamRecord dataclasses + Competition constants\n│   ├── normalize.py                # accents/date/name normalization helpers\n│   ├── clubs.py                    # curated Brazilian club registry (aliases, FIFA names, derbies)\n│   ├── loaders.py                  # load all 6 CSVs → matches, players\n│   ├── knowledge_graph.py          # in-memory property graph builder + queries\n│   ├── engine.py                   # SoccerData: indexes + all query methods (formatted output)\n├── tests/\n│   ├── conftest.py                 # session-scoped fixture loading SoccerData\n│   ├── test_normalization.py       # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_match_queries.py       # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_team_queries.py        # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_player_queries.py      # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_competition_queries.py # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_statistics.py          # BDD GWT\n│   ├── test_knowledge_graph.py     # BDD GWT\n│   └── test_mcp_server.py          # tool wiring + MCP protocol smoke test\n├── requirements.txt\n├── pyproject.toml                  # pytest config\n└── README.md                       # update with usage\n```\n\nEvery code file gets a \"detailed context block comment\" at the top? The prompts.txt variant mentions that instruction, but TASK.md doesn't. The user instruction says \"Read TASK.md ... implement everything it asks for\". TASK.md doesn't require context block comments. But CLAUDE.md/system says don't add comments unless asked... The TASK.md spec doesn't ask. I'll add concise module docstrings (not per-huge-comment blocks) — docstrings are standard and good practice, and the TASK says \"Maintain a detailed context block\"? No it doesn't — that's prompts.txt which I'm told to ignore. I'll use module docstrings + minimal comments where genuinely needed. Actually, a brief module-level docstring on each file is good documentation practice and expected for a quality submission. Keep comments minimal per instructions.\n\n**Design details:**\n\n`models.py`:\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Match:\n    match_id: str            # stable: source + row number\n    date: date | None\n    time: str | None\n    competition: str        # canonical competition name, e.g. \"Brasileirão Série A\"\n    competition_family: str # \"serie_a\", \"copa_do_brasil\", \"libertadores\", \"serie_b\", \"serie_c\"\n    season: int | None\n    stage: str | None       # round/stage descriptor\n    round: int | None\n    home_team: str          # canonical club id\n    away_team: str\n    home_display: str       # pretty display name\n    away_display: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    source: str             # file the row came from\n    stats: MatchStats | None  # corners/shots/attacks/halftime for BRF rows\n```\n\nPlayer:\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Player:\n    fifa_id: int\n    name: str\n    age: int | None\n    nationality: str\n    overall: int\n    potential: int\n    club: str | None        # raw FIFA club string\n    club_canonical: str | None  # canonical club id if Brazilian club, else None\n    position: str | None\n    jersey: int | None\n    value_eur: int | None\n    wage_eur: int | None\n    preferred_foot: str | None\n```\n\n`normalize.py`:\n- `strip_accents`, `normalize_key(s)` (dots removed, punctuation→space, collapse)\n- `UF_CODES` frozenset\n- `split_state(key)` → (key_without_trailing_uf, uf or None)\n- `parse_date(s)` handling: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"2023-09-24\", \"29/03/2003\", plus \"DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM\"? novo is date-only DD/MM/YYYY. Return `date` or None; also `parse_datetime` returns (date, time_str).\n\n`clubs.py`:\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Club:\n    club_id: str\n    name: str              # display \"Flamengo\"\n    state: str | None       # \"RJ\"\n    country: str            # \"BRA\" or \"INT\" (foreign)\n    aliases: frozenset[str] # normalized keys incl. state forms + base\n    fifa_names: tuple[str, ...]  # exact FIFA Club column strings\n\nCLUBS: dict[str, Club]  # id → club\nALIAS_INDEX: dict[str, str]  # normalized key → club_id\nBASE_INDEX: dict[str, str]  # base (uf-stripped) key → club_id for major clubs\nDERBIES: list[Derby]  # (name, club_a, club_b)\n```\n\nCurated club list (Brazilian clubs appearing across files — I'll include ~60):\n\nSérie A majors + cross-file + FIFA-mapped:\n1. flamengo RJ: flamengo, flamengo rj, cr flamengo\n2. fluminense RJ: fluminense, fluminense rj\n3. vasco RJ: vasco, vasco rj, vasco da gama, vasco da gama rj, cr vasco da gama\n4. botafogo_rj: botafogo, botafogo rj, botafogo de futebol e regatas\n5. corinthians SP: corinthians, corinthians sp, sport club corinthians paulista\n6. palmeiras SP: palmeiras, palmeiras sp, se palmeiras\n7. sao_paulo SP: sao paulo, sao paulo sp\n8. santos SP: santos, santos sp, santos fc\n9. gremio RS: gremio, gremio rs\n10. internacional RS: internacional, internacional rs, sc internacional\n11. atletico_mg MG: atletico mg, atletico mineiro, clube atletico mineiro\n12. cruzeiro MG: cruzeiro, cruzeiro mg\n13. atletico_pr PR (Athletico Paranaense): atletico pr, athletico pr, atletico paranaense, athletico paranaense, athletico, atlético paranaense... also \"ca parana\"? No — CA Parana = Paraná Clube? Hmm wait: BR-Football \"CA Parana\" (1) — CA Paranaense is Athletico's legal name (Clube Atlético Paranaense). And \"Paraná\" the club is \"Paraná Clube\". Hmm: \"CA Parana\" 1 occurrence... Ambiguous; Parana Clube is \"Paraná\". I'll map \"ca parana\" → atletico_pr (Atlético Paranaense's formal name is Clube Atlético Paranaense). Hmm, but wait — Paran