s \"Example answer format\" shows formatted text answers. My engine returns dict with \"summary\" (formatted text) + structured fields. Tools return that dict as JSON. LLM clients get both. \n\nTests: use `mcp` client? The SDK ships `mcp.client` for in-memory testing via streams. In MCP 2.x, what's the client API? Let me check quickly. There should be session objects. Simpler: test tools by calling the engine functions directly + test server tool functions as plain functions (they're just python functions before decoration). For a protocol-level smoke test, use the 2.x client if easy; else test `MCPServer.list_tools()`/`call_tool()` directly as methods! MCPServer has `list_tools` and `call_tool` as plain async methods — I can call them in pytest with asyncio.run. That's a genuine MCP server-level test without transports. \n\nBDD GWT style: pytest classes with Given/When/Then structure. Without pytest-bdd installed (can't rely on network? network worked for pip... let me try installing pytest-bdd. Actually — the venv pip install mcp worked, so network is available. But requirements should stay minimal & robust: I'll write GWT-style tests in plain pytest (class-based: `class TestFindMatchesBetweenTwoTeams: def given_... def when_... def then_...`)? Hmm — pytest collects test methods; GWT phrasing can be done via method names: `test_given_loaded_data_when_search_flamengo_fluminense_then_matches_returned`. Cleaner: docstrings with Gherkin (`\"\"\"Given ... When ... Then ...\"\"\"`) + assertion methods named naturally. I'll structure each test module as Feature (module docstring), each test function as Scenario (docstring with Given/When/Then lines), with local helper variables named given/when/then where natural. This is BDD GWT structured pytest without the pytest-bdd dependency. \n\nHmm, let me check if pytest-bdd is installable — it would be more literal Gherkin. The spec says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" with a Gherkin example. pytest-bdd