 simplify later if needed. Actually let me simplify now for readability... it works; keep.\n\nOne issue in `search_matches`: when `family` is set AND season is set, pool = by_family_season — fine. When only season set: pool filter includes `season is not None` — wait I wrote:\n\n```python\npool = self.matches\nif family:\n    pool = self._by_family.get(family, [])\nelif season is not None:\n    pool = [m for m in pool if m.season == season]\nif family and season is not None:\n    pool = self._by_family_season.get((family, season), [])\n```\n\nIf season set and family None → filter ✓. If both → by_family_season ✓. If family only ✓. OK.\n\nAnother design consideration: `search_matches` with `team` resolved via `resolve_club(team)` — but user may pass a fuzzy name that resolves via candidates. `_require_team` returns error dict if unresolved. But then I call `resolve_club(team)` again — if team is None (no team filter), `resolve_club(None)`... I guard: `team_id = resolve_club(team).club_id if team else None` ✓.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: `_require_team` uses `self.resolve_team` which does exact → fuzzy candidates; only exact wins. For search_matches I only accept exact (good determinism), with candidate suggestions on failure. \n\n`stage` filter: \"final\" for Libertadores — stage values \"group stage\", \"round of 16\", \"quarterfinals\", \"semifinals\", \"final\". normalize_text(\"final\") in normalize_text(\"final\") ✓. But note \"round of 16\" etc. Also searching stage \"semifinal\" in \"semifinals\" ✓ substring.\n\nNow — `competition_finals` for Copa do Brasil: my final-round detection requires the 2 matches in the max round to have {home,away} == {home,away} (same pairing reversed). Round 8 of 2019: Athletico x Inter, Inter x Athletico ✓. But what if the final round has 2 matches that are NOT the same pairing (data issues)? Then final_round stays None for that round and I `break`... no wait, I iterate rounds descending and check each; if the max round fails the check I continue to lower rounds. Hmm — `for rnd in sorted(rounds, reverse=True)`: I check round 8; if not a two-leg same-pairing, check round 7 etc. That could match a semifinal wrongly (round 7 has 4 matches — len(games)==2 fails, so skipped ✓). Only rounds with exactly 2 same-pairing matches qualify. A \"final\" that's a single match (none in this data) wouldn't be found — acceptable, documented behavior.\n\nWait, actually there's an issue: 2012 Cup rounds: {1:53, 2:29, 3:16, 4:8, 5:4, 6:2} — round 6 = 2 matches = final ✓. 2016: {1:75,...,7:2} — round 7 final ✓. 2021: {1:40, 2:20, 3:32, 4:16} — no 2-match round → no final ✓ (2021 final not in data).\n\nNow the standings source selection: `_standings_source` groups by `m.source` — but matches were DEDUPED, so each match has exactly one primary source ✓. For serie_a 2012-2019: Brasileirao (380) vs novo (380) — tie → priority: SOURCE_PRIORITY.index(\"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\")=0 < novo=3 → -SOURCE_PRIORITY.index → Brasileirao wins ✓.\n\nFor 2014-2021: BRF also has serie A rows — after dedup, e.g. 2015: Brasileirao 380 + BRF 378 (some may not dedupe due to date mismatches) → Brasileirao count 380 ≥ BRF → wins ✓. But if BRF had MORE distinct matches for a season than Brasileirao (e.g. some Brasileirao dates mismatched BRF dates creating 2 dedup groups), max-count picks the larger set. For 2012-2021, Brasileirao always has 380, BRF ≤ that. OK.\n\nFor serie_a 2022: only BRF → BRF source with 379 matches — partial note ✓. For 2023: BRF only ✓. For 2003-2011: novo only ✓.\n\nHmm — one more consideration on dedup between Brasileirao file and novo for 2012-2019: same fixtures (date, teams) — dedup groups them; primary = Brasileirao (stats None → key (False, -0) vs novo (False, -3) → Brasileirao wins). novo's Arena stadium info lost for those. Wait — the merge takes stage from others if primary lacks; stadium not merged. Minor. But actually — hold on, do novo and Brasileirao_Matches have the same dates for the same fixtures? If they differ (data entry), we'd get duplicates: same season+round+teams appearing twice → standings source selection still picks one source per season, so standings stay correct. Match searches might show near-duplicates for 2012-2019 if dates mismatch. Let me check how well novo/Brasileirao dates align for a common season, and how well BRF aligns with Brasileirao.\n\nActually, let me just quickly test the engine now — write a smoke script that loads everything and checks key numbers: total matches after dedup, 2019 standings, Flamengo-Fluminense H2H, etc. Then I'll know if dedup works.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b689a04001mMlq0cpDQO40Yj","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b686300001n0QbRcs9TY3YXG"}}
