e_pelotas'), '2019-03-07', 'Avai', 'Brasil de Pelotas')\n(('2019', 'x_mixto', 'chapecoense'), '2019-03-07', 'Mixto', 'Chapecoense')\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\nfrom soccer_mcp.clubs import resolve_club, fallback_club\nfrom soccer_mcp.normalize import parse_datetime\ndef rc(name): return (resolve_club(name) or fallback_club(name)).club_id\np='data/kaggle/'\nwith open(p+'BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    brf=[r for r in csv.DictReader(f) if r['tournament']=='Copa do Brasil']\nwith open(p+'Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    cup=[r for r in csv.DictReader(f)]\ncidx = {(r['season'], rc(r['home_team']), rc(r['away_team'])) for r in cup}\nunmatched = collections.Counter()\nsamples = []\nfor r in brf:\n    d = parse_datetime(r['date'])[0]\n    k = (str(d.year), rc(r['home']), rc(r['away']))\n    if k not in cidx:\n        unmatched[k[0]] += 1\n        if len(samples)<10 and k[0] in ('2018','2019'): samples.append((k, r['date'], r['home'], r['away']))\nprint(\"unmatched BRF cup rows by year:\", dict(sorted(unmatched.items())))\nprint(\"BRF cup rows by year:\", dict(sorted(collections.Counter(parse_datetime(r['date'])[0].year for r in brf).items())))\nprint(\"cup file rows by year:\", dict(sorted(collections.Counter(r['season'] for r in cup).items())))\nfor s in samples: print(s)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788240136048,"end":1788240136052}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Key findings:\n\n**Serie A (BRF vs Brasileirao file)**:\n- 3689 BRF Serie A rows total.\n- 417 rows are pairings NOT in the Brasileirao file for that season. Teams like coritiba/vasco/goias/botafogo_rj (2021 mislabels) and santos/fortaleza (2023 — Brasileirao file has no 2023; \"not in file\" because the file doesn't cover 2023 at all!). So the 417 = 2023 rows (377) + 2021 mislabeled (~45-ish minus... wait 417-377=40). Roughly.\n- Of matched pairings: 2426 exact same date, 773 ±1 day, ~65 with big diffs (50+ days) — those are probably wrong dates in one of the sources (postponed matches recorded at original date in one file, actual played date in the other? or rescheduled matches matched to the wrong leg! Since pairing (home,away) appears once per season per orientation, a big diff means genuinely different recorded dates — likely BRF records the actual date while Brasileirao file records a rescheduled one, or vice versa).\n\n**Copa do Brasil (BRF vs Cup file)**: 1123 BRF rows, 523 pairings not in cup file (BRF cup covers 2022-2023 which the cup file doesn't — 2022: 117, 2023: 116 = 233... plus cup file gaps), and of matched: 269 same-day, 327 ±1 day, 4 within 2-3 days.\n\nSo **date shifts of ±1 day are pervasive in BRF**. My dedup must handle ±1 day. Big diffs (100+ days, ~65 rows) — those pairings exist in both files but with very different dates. If I merge with ±1 tolerance only, those 65 remain as duplicates (both files' versions kept). ~65 duplicate fixtures across 2014-2023 Serie A — a small residue, acceptable... but wait, could they be REAL different matches? Same (season, home, away) orientation appearing twice in Serie A with different dates months apart — impossible in a double round-robin (each orientation occurs exactly once). So they're the same fixture with divergent recorded dates. 65 rows → 65 extra duplicate matches polluting Serie A stats for those seasons.\n\nHmm. What causes such divergence? Probably BRF records the SCHEDULED date while the Brasileirao file records the actual (postponed) date, or one file has plain errors. Which is right? Can't know. I'll merge them too — merge ALL same (family, season, home, away) orientation pairs across sources when their dates are within a threshold... If threshold = ∞ (ignore date entirely), Serie C's 4-meeting problem appears (BRF-only family, within-source duplicates never occur — wait, within BRF Serie C, could the same (season, home, away) orientation appear twice legitimately? Serie C format: 20 clubs in 2 groups of 10, playing group opponents 4 times (2 home, 2 away) — so YES, same orientation twice, and even 4 total meetings. Serie C is in BRF ONLY, so those would be different rows, same orientation, different dates → merging with ∞ threshold would destroy legit matches!).\n\nSo threshold approach: merge only rows from DIFFERENT sources with same (family, season, home, away) when dates are within N days. What N? For Serie A: 773 at 1 day, 65 at >50 days. A threshold of 3 days captures 3199+773+4... = 3199 of 3266; leaves ~65 as separate. A threshold of 400 days would capture all but risks Serie C... no wait — Serie C only has BRF rows; different-source condition never triggers (only one source). Copa do Brasil: same-orientation twice within a season? Round-of-16 two legs are REVERSED orientation ✓ distinct. Same orientation twice in a cup season: doesn't happen in these cups (each round two-legged with reversed venues). Libertadores: BRF has no Libertadores ✓. Serie B: double round robin, one orientation per pairing ✓.\n\nSo for cross-source merging, a LARGE threshold is safe for all these families EXCEPT it would also merge genuinely different matches... e.g., Serie A team A hosts B once. Two sources both have that orientation → same fixture. With threshold ∞ (i.e., merge all cross-source same-orientation rows), the only risk is cup same-orientation repeats — which don't exist in Copa do Brasil (BRF cup rows matched by pairing+season to cup file: those 523 \"not in file\" — 2022/2023 + rounds cup file lacks).\n\nWait, actually, hmm: BRF \"Copa do Brasil\" 2022/2023 vs Cup file ending 2021 — no overlap → cross-merge never triggers ✓.\n\nBut there IS one more subtlety: **within-source exact duplicates**. BRF 2021 Serie A: 491 unique fixtures by (date, teams) — 0 dups. OK, and by (season, home, away)? The Vasco/Coritiba rows were MISLABELED Serie B matches — those pairings appear in both BRF \"Serie A\" 2021 AND BRF \"Serie B\" 2021 (probably) — different families → stay separate. Mislabeling persists. Can I detect? Serie A 2021: valid teams = 20 from Brasileirao file. BRF rows with teams outside that set = mislabeled. I'll implement the **authoritative-season filter**: for (family, season) where a preferred source exists (Brasileirao/novo for serie_a; Cup file for copa_do_brasil), drop non-preferred-source rows involving teams that never appear in the preferred source for that season... Hmm wait, but that drops LEGIT matches too? For serie_a 2012-2022, the Brasileirao file is complete (380/season) — any BRF row not matching a preferred fixture is either date-shifted (merge), mislabeled (drop ✓), or missing from the preferred file (preferred files are complete, so none).\n\nActually the cleanest formulation: **cross-source merge with season-pairing key (no date), keeping the preferred source's date and stage; then drop residual BRF rows for (family, season) where the preferred file has full coverage**. \"Full coverage\" = preferred file has ≥ the expected number of fixtures... complicated.\n\nSimplest robust algorithm achieving all goals:\n\nFor each (family, season):\n  1. Partition rows by source.\n  2. If a preferred source exists for this family (Brasileirao/novo for serie_a, Cup file for copa_do_brasil):\n     - The preferred rows define the fixture set.\n     - For each non-preferred row: if its (home, away) pairing (orientation-specific) exists among preferred rows for that season → merge into it (attach stats; keep preferred date/stage). Else → DROP the row (mislabeled or the preferred file is complete anyway).\n       Wait — careful: BRF serie_a 2023 rows have NO preferred file → nothing dropped, all kept (2023 only in BRF). For serie_a 2012-2022: BRF rows whose orientation-pairing exists in Brasileirao → merged with stats ✓; BRF rows with pairings NOT in Brasileirao (Vasco 2021 mislabels) → dropped ✓. \n       But hold on — is Brasileirao 2012-2022 truly complete for every season? 380 × 11 = 4180 ✓ complete double round-robin every season. And novo 2003-2019: complete? 6886 rows / 17 years ≈ 405 avg — some seasons have more teams (24-team 2003: 552; 22-team 2004-5: 462...). novo is complete for its seasons (it's a well-known clean dataset). And cup file 2012-2021: complete cup records? 1337 rows; a complete Copa do Brasil 2012-2021 would have ~80-100 matches/season × 10 = ~900... plus extra rounds; the cup file is what it is — for copa 2012-2021 it's authoritative. BRF cup rows for those seasons with pairings in cup file → merged; not in cup file → hmm, should I drop? The cup file might be missing early-round upsets? If BRF has a 2018 cup match the cup file lacks, dropping loses data. Risk assessment: cup file rounds per season look complete-ish (40+20+20+10+16+8+4+2=120 for 2019 — real 2019 Copa do Brasil had 80 teams, 87 matches total... eh 120 ≥ 87 fine).\n       For cup, dropping unmatched BRF rows could lose data, but keeping them pollutes. Given the spec prioritizes the named Kaggle files (\"The following datasets ... must be used\"), treating family-specific files as authoritative is defensible. I'll drop-and-count, reporting dropped rows in a `data_quality` note. Actually — simpler decision: for copa_do_brasil seasons covered by the Cup file, drop unmatched BRF rows (mislabels); the Cup file is the canonical source per the spec.\n\n  3. If NO preferred source exists (serie_b, serie_c, serie_a 2023, copa 2022-2023, libertadores always): keep BRF rows as-is; within-family dedup by (date, home, away) exact (already unique).\n\nThis gives:\n- Serie A 2003-2022: Brasileirao/novo fixtures + BRF stats merged in where pairings match. Complete, no dupes, no mislabels. \n- Serie A 2023: BRF 377 rows (partial, flagged).\n- Serie B/C: BRF only ✓.\n- Copa 2012-2021: Cup file + BRF stats merged ✓. Copa 2022-2023: BRF only ✓.\n- Libertadores: Lib file only ✓ (no BRF Libertadores).\n\nAlso novo vs Brasileirao overlap 2012-2019: same treatment — 