antinopolis: [('tocantinopolis', None), ('tocantinopolis to', 'TO')]\n  treze: [('treze', None), ('treze pb', 'PB')]\n  tubarao: [('tubarao', None), ('tubarao sc', 'SC')]\n  uberlandia: [('uberlandia', None), ('uberlandia mg', 'MG')]\n  urt: [('urt', None), ('urt mg', 'MG')]\n  villa nova: [('villa nova', None), ('villa nova mg', 'MG')]\n  volta redonda: [('volta redonda', None), ('volta redonda rj', 'RJ')]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\nfrom soccer_mcp.clubs import resolve_club\nfrom soccer_mcp.normalize import normalize_text, split_state_suffix\np='data/kaggle/'\nraws = collections.Counter()\nfiles = [('Brasileirao_Matches.csv',('home_team','away_team')),\n         ('Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv',('home_team','away_team')),\n         ('Libertadores_Matches.csv',('home_team','away_team')),\n         ('BR-Football-Dataset.csv',('home','away')),\n         ('novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv',('Equipe_mandante','Equipe_visitante'))]\nfor fn, cols in files:\n    with open(p+fn, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n            for c in cols: raws[r[c]] += 1\n\n# group raw names by base key (UF-stripped), excluding curated resolutions\nbases = collections.defaultdict(set)\nfor raw in raws:\n    if resolve_club(raw) is not None: continue\n    key = normalize_text(raw)\n    base, uf = split_state_suffix(key)\n    bases[base].add((raw, key, uf))\n\ncoll = {b: v for b, v in bases.items() if len({(k,u) for _,k,u in v}) > 1 and len(b.split())<=3}\nprint(f\"{len(coll)} collision bases\")\nfor b in sorted(coll):\n    vs = sorted({(k,u) for _,k,u in coll[b]})\n    if len(vs) > 1:\n        print(f\"  {b}: {vs}\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788240152013,"end":1788240152017}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I see! The unmatched BRF cup rows for 2019 involve teams like \"Luverdense\", \"Aparecidense\", \"Moto Club de São Luís\", \"Mixto\", \"Brasil de Pelotas\" — these are REAL 2019 Copa do Brasil second-round teams. The Cup file 2019 has round 1 = 40 matches... and rounds 2-8. Total 120. But BRF has 117. Yet ~40 BRF pairings/season don't match cup file pairings.\n\nWait — Cup file 2019 round 1: 40 matches. Real 2019 Copa do Brasil first round had 40 matches ✓. Hmm, so why doesn't \"Avai vs Brasil de Pelotas\" (BRF 2019-03-07) appear in the Cup file?\n\nOH WAIT. I see it — the Cup file probably has these matches under DIFFERENT team spellings that resolve to different club ids! E.g. Cup file has \"Luverdense - MT\" → key \"luverdense mt\" → not curated → fallback id \"x_luverdense_mt\" ≠ BRF \"Luverdense\" → \"x_luverdense\". The fallback ids differ because of the state suffix! \"Luverdense - MT\" (cup) → x_luverdense_mt; \"Luverdense\" (BRF) → x_luverdense. Same club, different identities!\n\nSo my fallback identity system breaks pairing matching for uncurated small clubs with state suffixes in one file but not the other. The Cup file appends \" - MT\" to EVERY team. BRF drops states for teams without ambiguity.\n\nFix: make the fallback identity STATE-INSENSITIVE by default: fallback key = normalized name with the trailing UF stripped (base), so \"Luverdense - MT\" → base \"luverdense\" == BRF \"Luverdense\" ✓. But then clubs like \"Sao Jose PA\" vs \"Sao Jose RS\" vs \"Sao Jose - POA\" would all collapse to \"sao jose\" — WRONG (different clubs).\n\nHmm. Balance: cross-file matching matters most for teams appearing in MULTIPLE files. For Serie A the majors are curated. For cup small clubs: Cup file (state suffix) vs BRF (no suffix) vs Cup file alone. \n\nOptions:\n1. Fallback identity keeps FULL key (current), and additionally maintain an alias resolution in the fallback space: when resolving a raw name, first try curated registry, then try a fallback-identity index keyed by BOTH full key and base key, preferring... the same ambiguity problem.\n\n2. State-stripped fallback identity + parenthetical kept + rely on curated aliases for the ambiguous cases (sao jose pa/rs, santa cruz rn/rs, etc.). The ambiguous small-club set: sao jose, santa cruz, botafogo (curated), nacional, river, ypiranga (curated rs), vitoria (curated)...\n\nLet me enumerate which base keys are shared by 2+ distinct raw teams across the files (after UF strip): that determines the collision set. For collisions, curate explicit entries; for the rest, state-insensitive fallback is safe.\n\nLet me compute: all raw team names across the 5 match files → fallback base key (strip UF) → set of raw names per base key. Where a base key maps to multiple DISTINCT raw spellings that are plausibly the same club (e.g. \"luverdense mt\" + \"luverdense\") vs genuinely different clubs (\"sao jose pa\" + \"sao jose rs\"). The genuinely-different ones I must curate or keep distinct.\n\nPractical approach: compute collision bases; for each, decide:\n- Same club variants → fine, one fallback id on the base.\n- Different clubs → need distinct ids → curate entries with explicit aliases claiming their state-suffixed keys, and make the fallback use the FULL key when the base is \"claimed\" by multiple curated clubs...\n\nThis is getting deep. Simpler alternative: **fallback identity = base key (state-stripped); ambiguity handled by preferring curated registry on resolve; and for genuinely distinct same-base clubs, add curated entries** (like I did for botafogo_pb/sp, sao_jose...). Let me enumerate the collision set now and see how big it is.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b6b3ac8001rQQSahTllh35kc","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b6b08ce001U2667jU740HnxK"}}
