w_name.strip()\nif uf:\n    # strip trailing state marker from display\n    m = re.match(r\"^(.*?)\\s*[-–]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\s*$\", raw_name.strip())  # case-insensitive-ish\n    if m: display = m.group(1).strip()\n    else:\n        m2 = re.match(r\"^(.*?)\\s*\\([A-Za-z]{2}\\)\\s*$\", raw_name.strip())  # \"(PI)\" style\n        ...\n```\nHmm — raw names with state: \"4 de Julho - PI\"? Actually BRF has \"4 de Julho EC\" (no state) and the Cup file has \"4 de Julho - PI\". The raw with state: \"4 de Julho - PI\" → strip \" - PI\" → \"4 de Julho\". Also \"River (PI)\" → strip \"(PI)\" → \"River\". Use regexes on the RAW string before normalization: remove trailing \" - XX\" where XX is 2 uppercase-ish letters (the raw uses proper case \"PI\"), and trailing \"(XX)\".\n\nActually simpler: use the base-key words to reconstruct? No — accents lost. Do raw-string regex:\n```python\nraw = raw_name.strip()\nstate_re = re.search(r\"^(.*?)[\\s]*[-–]\\s*([A-Za-z]{2})$\", raw)\nparen_re = re.search(r\"^(.*?)\\s*\\(([A-Za-z]{2})\\)$\", raw)\n```\nif uf matches the captured state (upper) → display = prefix.\n\nEdge: \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" — curated, not fallback. OK.\n\nLet me implement.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b6d78cc001wSj30pndwRmrG0","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b6d0dc0001BHq746f9cuSIoz"}}
