`parsars.parse(..., extra_types)` it's fiddly. Simplest: reorder the two step definitions so the LONGER pattern (with competition) is registered first? Does pytest-bdd match steps in definition order? pytest-bdd collects step definitions per name string — for parsers.parse, it tries to match the step text against each registered parser; first match wins in registration order. My `when_search_team_season_only` was defined BEFORE `when_search_team_season`? No wait — in my file, `when_search_team_season` (with competition) is defined first, then `when_search_team_season_only`. But the error shows the ONLY pattern matched. Hmm — pytest-bdd might match in reverse order or by specificity... The error shows team='Palmeiras\" in competition \"Série A' — the shorter pattern won.\n\nRobust fix: constrain `{team}` to non-quote characters in both patterns. pytest-bdd's parsers.parse uses parse library — `{team}` matches non-greedy any chars INCLUDING quotes. I can use custom format specs: `{{team:Word}}`? The parse library supports custom types... In pytest-bdd you can register extra types via `parsers.parse(pattern, extra_types={...})`. Simpler: use regex parsers for these two steps:\n\n```python\nfrom pytest_bdd import parsers\n@when(parsers.re(r'I search for matches of \"(?P<team>[^\"]+)\" in competition \"(?P<competition>[^\"]+)\" in season (?P<season>\\d+)'), target_fixture=\"result\")\n```\n\nRegex with `[^\"]+` is unambiguous. Let me convert both `when_search_team_season` and `when_search_team_season_only` to regex parsers with non-quote team captures.\n\nAlso fix the Neymar steps with quoted names, and the Flamengu fuzzy candidates (difflib).\n\nLet me make these three fixes.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b71f982001gqIb7wz4fbJMdS","sessionID":"ses_fa4a24ca1ffeh7JKx4QpKQV4BC","messageID":"msg_05b71cae5001hPPY7gqRW2fCsJ"}}
